Ladybird makes a manifest version 2 background page persistent unless
background.persistent is false, and treats every version 3 page as
nonpersistent, ignoring the key there with a logged warning. Chromium’s
BackgroundInfo::LoadBackgroundPersistent() reads the same key for page
backgrounds, and rejects a value that isn’t a boolean, as Ladybird does;
its version 3 backgrounds are service workers, so it has no page case to
compare. Gecko’s Extension.persistentBackground makes version 3
backgrounds nonpersistent and ignores the key there with a warning,
which is the behavior Ladybird follows.

The 30-second idle timeout follows Gecko (the
extensions.background.idle.timeout preference) and WebKit
(WebExtensionContext::scheduleBackgroundContentToUnload(), 30 seconds
outside its test runner); Chromium’s ProcessManager uses
g_event_page_idle_time, 10 seconds. The bounded keepalive for version 3
pages follows Chromium’s service-worker direction and Gecko’s
IdleManager, which keeps a page only for a bounded time on pending work,
rather than WebKit’s two-minute port grace period.
